# Every checkable row in the corpus, with its verdict. One row per (rule, examined item): which
# rule looked at it, which layer of which filing it is, whether it breaches the rule, and a
# sentence saying what it says.
#
# One relation, two views
#   This is the relation the violations table and the coverage table are both views of, and that
#   is the entire point of assembling it. A violations table is `WHERE violates`; a coverage
#   table is `GROUP BY rule`. Because they read one relation, a coverage number cannot be about a
#   different population than the rule it reports on. That is what happens when each population
#   is written twice, in two dialects of itself: the restatements disagree, and one counting four
#   rows where its rule examines one crosses a threshold and reports a thin rule as well covered.
#
# The most useful thing here to run on its own
#   An empty violations table is the good outcome and tells a reader nothing at all. This shows
#   every rule working on real rows: what it looked at, what each row said, and why it passed.
#   That is the only way to see that a rule is doing something rather than merely finding
#   nothing.
#
# A rule that examines nothing still appears
#   It appears with a NULL verdict. That is not a quirk; it is the reason every check starts by
#   joining checks/roster.sqlc. A check that merely filtered its population would vanish from a
#   `GROUP BY` when the population was empty, so the one verdict that matters most, vacuous, is
#   precisely the one a grouping cannot produce. The roster row exists before the population
#   does, so the rule reports that it ran even when it had nothing to look at.
#
# The contract every check keeps
#   One plain SELECT, no leading WITH, returning exactly `(rule, filing, layer, violates,
#   detail)`, with `violates` NULL only on the row that says a rule examined nothing. A leading
#   WITH cannot appear after UNION ALL, so a check needing recursion puts the walk in its own
#   relation under composition/ and joins it, which is better anyway, because the walk is then
#   runnable by itself.
-- the conformance rules XSD 1.0 cannot reach, one file each; checks/roster.sqlc is the list.
:union(ALL checks/fit_disagrees.sqlc,
           checks/shares_do_not_sum.sqlc,
           checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude.sqlc,
           checks/nobody_named_as_unserved.sqlc,
           checks/exposure_unaccounted.sqlc,
           checks/share_exceeds_slack.sqlc,
           checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc,
           checks/quantum_unit_mismatch.sqlc,
           checks/nameplate_not_a_multiple.sqlc,
           checks/draw_exceeds_the_supply.sqlc,
           checks/clearance_with_unserved.sqlc,
           checks/unresolved_part.sqlc,
           checks/jagged_layer.sqlc,
           checks/layers_move_together.sqlc,
           checks/coupling_does_not_attenuate.sqlc,
           checks/narrows_a_point_value.sqlc,
           checks/range_says_no_range.sqlc,
           checks/bound_fell_with_no_range.sqlc,
           checks/identity_does_not_compute_the_claim.sqlc,
           checks/window_lost_or_summed.sqlc,
           checks/derived_slack_over_a_window.sqlc,
           checks/window_not_applicable_on_a_rate.sqlc,
           checks/window_size_not_applicable.sqlc,
           checks/elimination_not_applicable_with_parts.sqlc,
           checks/fusion_sum_disagrees.sqlc,
           checks/local_part_dangles.sqlc,
           checks/denied_remainder_is_not_contradicted.sqlc,
           checks/one_part_fusion_alters_its_part.sqlc,
           checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close.sqlc,
           checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor.sqlc,
           checks/regime_crossing_without_a_citation.sqlc,
           checks/part_regime_disagrees.sqlc)
